{
  "_comment": [
      "Locale tag canonicalisation, likely subtags, parent locales, fallback chains, resolution and per-key lookup. Rules: SPEC.md, sections Locale tags and Fallback.",
      "Sources: BCP 47 (RFC 5646) syntax; CLDR supplemental aliases.json, likelySubtags.json and parentLocales.json; UTS 35 part 1 (locale inheritance). Expected values computed with ICU 79.1 / CLDR 49 (ECMAScript Intl.getCanonicalLocales, Intl.Locale.maximize) and from the cldr-json files of CLDR 47, 48 and 49 (parent locales, likely subtags). PHP ext-intl Locale::canonicalize is not a reference: it does not apply aliases or reject malformed tags.",
      "`since`: first CLDR major in which the row holds. A port on an older CLDR (47 is the minimum) skips rows whose `since` exceeds its CLDR version; a port on that version or later MUST pass them. Rows without `since` are identical in CLDR 47, 48 and 49.",
      "canonicalize: `in` -> `out`, or `error` (always invalid_locale). A `_` separator is read as `-` first; any other non-BCP 47 syntax is rejected.",
      "likely_subtags: `tag` -> `max`, the CLDR likely-subtags expansion (language, script and region all present).",
      "parent: CLDR locale inheritance, one step. `parent` is null when the next step is root (nothing further to try).",
      "chain: locales to try for `tag`, then for `default`, de-duplicated, first occurrence kept. Per tag: the canonical tag without extension and private-use subtags; then its normal form (a script equal to the language likely script dropped, a script missing but implied by language+region likely subtags added: zh-TW becomes zh-Hant-TW, en-Latn-US becomes en-US); then parents until root.",
      "resolve: first chain entry present in `available` (canonical comparison); null when none. `error` = invalid_locale for a malformed tag or default.",
      "lookup: for each `keys` entry, the first chain entry (as for chain, over the locales of `catalogs`) whose catalog defines the key; null when none does. `catalogs` maps locale to the dotted keys it defines."
  ],
